    "case_id": "12",
    "case_summary": "J.R. was a 3210 gram infant born to a 21-year-old first-time mother via vaginal delivery at 36 weeks of gestation. The mother had regular prenatal care, and the pregnancy was without complications. There was no history of tobacco, alcohol, illicit substance, or medication use during the pregnancy. A routine prenatal ultrasound identified a cardiac echogenic focus and a potential anatomical heart defect, leading to the child's transfer to the neonatal intensive care unit at birth. A thoracic echocardiogram revealed mitral stenosis, aortic stenosis, and coarctation of the aorta. Surgical intervention was performed to repair the coarctation, and a right diaphragmatic eventration was also corrected during the same procedure. No cardiac arrhythmias were detected on telemetry during the initial hospitalization.\n\nA chromosome analysis including fluorescence in situ hybridization for both DiGeorge loci revealed no abnormalities.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
